WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:16.170
The inmates have overtaken many prisons in Venezuela.

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:18.790
They're armed with heavy weapons and grenades.

00:00:30.590 --> 00:00:36.140
The prisons are governed by criminal gangs led by a pran or kingpin who strictly enforces the thug code

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:40.350
by which all prisoners must abide or they will be shot in various body parts.

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:43.030
It's too dangerous for Venezuelan troops to enter

00:00:43.030 --> 00:00:47.245
so they patrol the perimeter and train their rifles on any inmate who tries to leave.

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:58.200
The prisoners have formed functional independent societies with open air bazaars

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:00.960
offering everything from Coca-Cola to cocaine.

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:04.331
Several days a week, they welcome their girlfriends, wives, children,

00:01:04.331 --> 00:01:09.014
and extended families for visits, birthday parties and even festivals.

00:01:13.817 --> 00:01:17.099
"La Causa," a new documentary from 29 year old filmmaker,

00:01:17.099 --> 00:01:20.400
Andrés Figueredo Thomson, is a raw look at life inside

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:23.340
what at the time of filming was the country's largest prison.

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:26.410
The film explores the structure of its self-organized society

00:01:26.410 --> 00:01:30.000
where dissenters and those deemed social radicals were treated harshly 

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.244
and LGBT inmates were cast out and forced to live on the roof of a building.

00:01:39.438 --> 00:01:42.210
Figueredo Thomson started capturing remarkable footage

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:45.250
in the prison in 2010 as the socialist strongman president,

00:01:45.250 --> 00:01:48.170
Hugo Chavez, dismantled democratic institutions

00:01:48.170 --> 00:01:50.370
and seized control of private businesses.

00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:53.851
The year after Figueredo Thomson began production on "La Causa," 

00:01:53.851 --> 00:01:58.820
Chavez declared his stepfather an enemy of the state, forcing his family to flee to the US.

00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:03.160
But he continued filming on return trips to Venezuela over the following eight years.

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:05.697
Senior year in high school was the first time I entered a prison.

00:02:05.697 --> 00:02:10.930
The experience was incredibly, incredibly, just overwhelming,

00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:14.265
seeing inmates with weapons, with grenades, the smell, 

00:02:14.265 --> 00:02:16.710
seeing literally excrement coming from the walls,

00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:22.192
just the amount of open sores everywhere, absolutely deplorable conditions.

00:02:22.192 --> 00:02:27.167
It felt almost like a war zone, like post-apocalyptic thing, to see the children...

00:02:27.167 --> 00:02:29.010
children going in and out of the prison.

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:33.720
The underbelly of crime in Venezuela, being around guns, being around crack.

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:39.304
That guy that was selling crack and cocaine with the baby in his arms.

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:50.030
You filmed this over the course of eight years.

00:02:50.030 --> 00:02:55.052
How did you start going into prisons in Venezuela when you were a senior in high school?

00:02:55.052 --> 00:02:57.508
I met this guy who was in the documentary,

00:02:57.508 --> 00:02:59.920
Gilber Carbo, he's a politician guy.

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:04.400
Me and my brother had an NGO and we used to build recreational parks in the slums.

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:07.320
And he told me the story of when he was a kingpin

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:09.576
and how he had transformed himself.

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:16.310
I was 17, and I told him, man, I want to do a documentary about this.

00:03:16.310 --> 00:03:19.760
I even had to make a fake ID to be able to get into the prison.

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:24.780
How did you ingratiate yourself in this community that they were willing to let you film them?

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:27.000
They always knew what was going on and they were okay with it.

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:32.099
Because I think there's also that element of pride, in some sense, 

00:03:32.099 --> 00:03:35.668
of being in that world or of wanting to show their life.

00:03:42.599 --> 00:03:47.740
But it was really interesting to see inside the power vacuum that they had filled.

00:03:47.740 --> 00:03:52.290
Before these kingpins came to power, it used to be like no man's land.

00:03:52.290 --> 00:03:55.069
And there used to be a lot more blood baths.

00:04:11.230 --> 00:04:13.330
With kingpins there was more selective violence.

00:04:13.330 --> 00:04:16.870
It was more like Hammurabi Code type of, an eye for an eye, type of thing.

00:04:16.870 --> 00:04:18.690
But you could leave your cell phone in the prison,

00:04:18.690 --> 00:04:21.590
and if your cell phone got lost somebody was gonna be responsible. 

00:04:21.590 --> 00:04:27.860
It was really hard for you—I would leave my wallet or my things just in the music studio that we built

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:31.900
and nothing would disappear in a place where it's surrounded by thieves.

00:04:31.900 --> 00:04:37.460
So there was this type of military dictatorship code which was really weird.

00:04:37.460 --> 00:04:43.024
I'm not saying that I support it, but it was a type of system that created some type of order

00:04:43.024 --> 00:04:44.660
from the previous chaos.

00:04:59.850 --> 00:05:05.750
The pran, or kingpin, presides over a multi-tiered security apparatus that oversees an army of watchmen

00:05:05.750 --> 00:05:09.275
who roam the prison grounds and ensure inmates obey strict social codes 

00:05:09.275 --> 00:05:12.980
that regulate where they can go, how they can act, and what they can say.

00:05:12.980 --> 00:05:14.730
There are certain codes that you can't break.

00:05:14.730 --> 00:05:17.870
Like you can't use the word for milk because it can also be misinterpreted.

00:05:17.870 --> 00:05:20.810
And so you have to know all of these things that,

00:05:20.810 --> 00:05:24.210
if you say them then you're going to lose positions and your ranking.

00:05:24.210 --> 00:05:27.160
Coming new to prison, at first, you're most likely gonna be sent to the church.

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:29.693
And in church, they're gonna ask you who you are, why are you here?

00:05:29.693 --> 00:05:31.600
What are you doing? Do you know anybody in here?

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:34.840
When prisoners first arrive, they're judged by the prison government

00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:37.680
and sorted into one of three distinct social castes,

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:41.930
the thugs, the evangelicals, or the renegades.

00:05:41.930 --> 00:05:44.081
Almost like going into college.

00:05:44.081 --> 00:05:46.150
Like if you get into a frat or not get into a frat,

00:05:46.150 --> 00:05:49.360
depends on who you know, how cool you are, what you've done, type of thing.

00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:53.110
Like it's interesting to see that society has it's ways to self-organize

00:05:53.110 --> 00:05:56.620
at these different levels but it's essentially the same thing.

00:05:56.620 --> 00:06:01.200
The thug is the people who are the freest inside the prison.

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:04.120
The thugs can not wear a shirt.

00:06:04.120 --> 00:06:05.760
They can walk wherever they want to.

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:09.800
They can smoke weed, or do drugs in the prison.

00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:13.480
The evangelicals, if they're walking about they have to have a Bible in their hands

00:06:13.480 --> 00:06:14.640
and they have to wear a tie.

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:17.720
And they're also in charge of cleaning the prison

00:06:17.720 --> 00:06:19.500
or they're also in charge of cooking.

00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:21.890
The renegades, the ones who are worst off,

00:06:21.890 --> 00:06:23.840
these are like the prisoners within prison.

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:28.270
And it's either because you committed a very, very horrible crime, you're a homosexual,

00:06:28.270 --> 00:06:35.320
like a known homosexual, or you belong to a gang that was an enemy of the kingpin's gang.

00:06:35.320 --> 00:06:37.350
In addition to preaching the word of God

00:06:37.350 --> 00:06:40.370
the evangelicals are responsible for cleaning the raw sewage

00:06:40.370 --> 00:06:42.740
and refuse that cover the grounds.

00:06:42.740 --> 00:06:47.460
They live in the church, which acts as both a place of worship and a prison within the prison.

00:06:47.460 --> 00:06:51.782
Those who violate the thug code or owe a debt can also be sent to the church.

00:06:55.500 --> 00:06:57.860
It's like your last warning, type of punishment.

00:06:57.860 --> 00:07:02.040
If you're in the church and you're caught by the kingpin doing something,

00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:06.200
you're either going to get shot in the hand, corporal punishment some way,

00:07:06.200 --> 00:07:09.783
or you're gonna be sent to the renegades.

00:07:09.783 --> 00:07:11.350
You can't get out of that.

00:07:11.350 --> 00:07:17.400
Once you do that, any prison that you go to, you have a stain that is not washable.

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:20.550
You go to another prison or even the streets, everybody's going to know, that guy,

00:07:20.550 --> 00:07:22.520
that guy has a stain on him.

00:07:22.520 --> 00:07:26.280
Because you were given the opportunity of the church to reform yourself,

00:07:26.280 --> 00:07:30.870
to no longer be in the world of evil and turn good.

00:07:30.870 --> 00:07:35.640
The prison's defacto government collects a general tax known as la causa, or the cause,

00:07:35.640 --> 00:07:38.263
which it uses to purchase goods that come into the prison.

00:07:41.620 --> 00:07:44.100
If you behave, if you pay your causa,

00:07:44.100 --> 00:07:45.170
then nobody's gonna mess with you.

00:07:45.170 --> 00:07:48.980
But if you're outspoken, if you do things wrong, if you're a mischief person,

00:07:48.980 --> 00:07:56.853
if you're the debtor, if you're like a bad person for the good living within the prison then you're going to fare badly.

00:08:01.560 --> 00:08:05.073
The Venezuelan bolivar has been destroyed by hyperinflation, 

00:08:05.073 --> 00:08:07.650
but inside, prisoners have created a market system

00:08:07.650 --> 00:08:10.370
in which they've established their own ad hoc currencies,

00:08:10.370 --> 00:08:13.400
bartered goods and services, and use systems of credit.

00:08:13.400 --> 00:08:17.910
The currency that was circulating outside was no longer valid inside the prison.

00:08:17.910 --> 00:08:20.573
There was a toilet paper shortage at some point in the country.

00:08:20.573 --> 00:08:24.650
And you could still, or adding a bottle of main product that we used to make arepas,

00:08:24.650 --> 00:08:27.400
there were shortages outside, and inside the prison you could still find them.

00:08:27.400 --> 00:08:29.330
People would go inside the prison to shop.

00:08:29.330 --> 00:08:31.850
Inmates run restaurants inside the prison's walls,

00:08:31.850 --> 00:08:34.720
provide an alternative to the food offered by the government.

00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:39.010
Like there was this guy that I really enjoyed talking to, and it was the place that I always went to eat.

00:08:39.010 --> 00:08:41.320
He had his burger shop. His burgers were amazing.

00:08:41.320 --> 00:08:44.168
The prison food that the government gave them, it wasn't nearly enough,

00:08:44.168 --> 00:08:48.222
and it was just like rice and like soggy.

00:08:48.222 --> 00:08:51.480
I got amebiasis, like amoebas, twice.

00:08:51.480 --> 00:08:53.330
It was the worst experience of my life.

00:08:53.330 --> 00:08:57.220
And the food is the least of the problems created by the government for most inmates.

00:08:57.220 --> 00:09:00.804
I saw a skeleton and it was moving, I had to do a double take—

00:09:00.804 --> 00:09:02.280
I didn't know if it was fear or whatever.

00:09:02.280 --> 00:09:06.140
And then I looked closer and I saw it was a man who was literally dying.

00:09:06.140 --> 00:09:13.000
He was only bone and some skin hanging on his—and he had had tuberculosis for nine months.

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:15.970
He was an indigenous of the Wayuu community,

00:09:15.970 --> 00:09:19.560
it's like a native community that lives near Columbia.

00:09:19.560 --> 00:09:21.420
And he was there for stealing a watch.

00:09:21.420 --> 00:09:24.503
And he'd been in prison, I don't know, like two or three years.

00:09:24.503 --> 00:09:28.170
And the day, supposedly, the day that we got there, me and Gilber,

00:09:28.170 --> 00:09:33.080
was the day that the guy had his freedom and we tried to take him to a hospital.

00:09:33.080 --> 00:09:35.390
And he just died the next day.

00:09:35.390 --> 00:09:38.320
Figueredo Thomson says that only about 20% of prisoners

00:09:38.320 --> 00:09:40.620
have been formally sentenced for a crime.

00:09:40.620 --> 00:09:44.113
Others languish for years awaiting trials that may never come.

00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:10.830
From the Free Convict group, they were 15, 12 of them hadn't been sentenced.

00:10:10.830 --> 00:10:13.600
And a lot of them just started admitting to sentence

00:10:13.600 --> 00:10:15.970
because then the time in prison would move faster.

00:10:15.970 --> 00:10:19.125
Imagine a system in which you have to declare yourself guilty even if you're not 

00:10:19.125 --> 00:10:21.200
 just so the thing moves faster.

00:10:21.200 --> 00:10:26.030
The prison population has exploded in recent years as the regime of Hugo Chavez's successor,

00:10:26.030 --> 00:10:30.713
Nicolás Maduro, has cracked down on mostly poor Venezuelans for alleged petty offenses.

00:10:36.369 --> 00:10:38.250
There was no economic opportunity.

00:10:38.250 --> 00:10:42.120
A lot of them are kids, you know, like 18 year old guy who's part of the documentary,

00:10:42.120 --> 00:10:44.783
he doesn't tell the story that much, but it's the guy with the afro, Freedom,

00:10:44.783 --> 00:10:46.900
he was thrown in because he stole a cell phone,

00:10:46.900 --> 00:10:51.150
and he spent three years in a prison in the same space,

00:10:51.150 --> 00:10:55.105
being overruled by guys who had killed 15 people.

00:10:55.105 --> 00:10:59.460
There's no like maximum security prison, only for political prisons, mostly.

00:10:59.460 --> 00:11:03.940
So obviously when you throw somebody into that type of position, what are you creating?

00:11:03.940 --> 00:11:08.460
This guy has to survive and he has to pay the causa to these kingpins.

00:11:08.460 --> 00:11:11.680
And he has to find a way to make a living within these prison walls.

00:11:11.680 --> 00:11:16.494
You're essentially making him create networks of new crime and new ways in which to live.

00:11:33.720 --> 00:11:37.200
When society doesn't pay attention to culture of rehabilitation,

00:11:37.200 --> 00:11:39.051
then that's going to start to fester.

00:12:05.650 --> 00:12:09.330
The film builds to a dramatic finish as the military embarks in a campaign

00:12:09.330 --> 00:12:13.970
to retake several inmate run prisons setting up a showdown in 2016 at the prison

00:12:13.970 --> 00:12:16.598
where Figueredo Thompson shot most of his footage.

00:12:16.598 --> 00:12:20.239
Little by little, a lot of these prisons have been taken control of.

00:12:30.490 --> 00:12:32.530
The campaign presents the socialist government

00:12:32.530 --> 00:12:34.650
with both practical and moral dilemmas.

00:12:34.650 --> 00:12:38.930
Since overtaking heavily armed inmates requires mass bloodshed and inevitably,

00:12:38.930 --> 00:12:41.480
killed scores of the wrongfully incarcerated,

00:12:41.480 --> 00:12:44.010
Figueredo Thompson says officials can't reconcile

00:12:44.010 --> 00:12:46.440
these actions with their foundational ideology

00:12:46.440 --> 00:12:48.440
which preaches compassion and the creation

00:12:48.440 --> 00:12:52.870
of a new man whose main virtues are selflessness and a commitment to justice.

00:12:52.870 --> 00:12:57.140
What they call their version of revolutionary socialism,

00:12:57.140 --> 00:13:02.865
how are you going to execute inmates if you're supposed to believe in the new man? 

00:13:02.865 --> 00:13:06.890
"El hombre nuevo—their philosophy, this is their philosophy, that Che Guevara bring in.

00:13:06.890 --> 00:13:11.510
You need the new man to reform,

00:13:11.510 --> 00:13:15.370
and use the prisons as a way to reeducate and et cetera, et cetera.

00:13:15.370 --> 00:13:17.690
How do you get to that point?

00:13:17.690 --> 00:13:19.950
Venezuela's slide into authoritarianism

00:13:19.950 --> 00:13:23.279
is also deeply personal for Figueredo Thompson and his family,

00:13:23.279 --> 00:13:28.010
his stepfather, Guillermo Zuloaga, led the last independent TV station in the country

00:13:28.010 --> 00:13:30.400
after Chavez seized most media outlets.

00:13:30.400 --> 00:13:33.130
He fought escalating government harassment for years

00:13:33.130 --> 00:13:36.660
as he exposed the vast human cost of the regime's policies.

00:13:36.660 --> 00:13:39.810
In 2010 Chavez finally had Zuloaga arrested.

00:13:39.810 --> 00:13:42.410
He had to leave the country because Chavez named him

00:13:42.410 --> 00:13:44.610
a political enemy of the revolution.

00:13:44.610 --> 00:13:47.658
My first year of university was in Venezuela.

00:13:47.658 --> 00:13:51.910
That was the year—right before that was the year that my stepfather had to leave,

00:13:51.910 --> 00:13:55.730
my family had to leave and I was left there alone with my younger brother, Juan.

00:13:55.730 --> 00:14:00.850
It's really strange to see when a man that you've seen your entire life be a completely moral man

00:14:00.850 --> 00:14:03.790
be thrown out of his country for speaking the truth.

00:14:03.790 --> 00:14:07.430
If you didn't question your beliefs, if you're dogmatic about your beliefs

00:14:07.430 --> 00:14:09.840
then there's no room for you to admit error.

00:14:09.840 --> 00:14:11.940
And I think that's a lot of what's happening in Venezuela

00:14:11.940 --> 00:14:16.450
is that there is no admittance of error and they've gone so far down a path

00:14:16.450 --> 00:14:21.870
that it's very hard to come back from when you become too radicalized in your ideological position.

00:14:21.870 --> 00:14:25.410
And I think that's something that I've been able to witness

00:14:25.410 --> 00:14:28.046
and to see firsthand, and it's very saddening.

00:14:43.247 --> 00:14:46.379
"La Causa" shines a light on the decline of Venezuelan society 

00:14:46.379 --> 00:14:49.730
and the human rights abuses perpetrated under Chávez and Maduro

00:14:49.730 --> 00:14:52.180
as their socialist revolution backpedals further

00:14:52.180 --> 00:14:56.938
from its promise to empower the downtrodden and celebrate the idea of the new man.

00:15:08.000 --> 00:15:11.020
But Figueredo Thomson's film also features poignant stories

00:15:11.020 --> 00:15:14.440
of individuals who have overcome the odds to find redemption

00:15:14.440 --> 00:15:16.950
whether or not they ultimately find justice.

00:15:38.840 --> 00:15:42.913
To be able to get to this level of access, to empathy and to understanding, 

00:15:42.913 --> 00:15:44.750
that was my experience.

00:15:44.750 --> 00:15:48.900
A lot of people are too afraid to go into the belly of the beast.

00:15:48.900 --> 00:15:54.410
And I think if you draw them a picture, starting to see things, generate empathy,

00:15:54.410 --> 00:15:56.504
and I think that's the power of film.

